      "rev_n_2_b9ab16_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_b9ab16_1",
        "node_id": "n_2_b9ab16",
        "thought_text": "Revised Thought-1: Ensure that the graph construction correctly handles cases where a participant prefers themselves, and adjust the logic to handle the bus capacity constraint properly.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_2_b9ab16_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_bf93ebb43b",
        "created_at": 1794505642.3206956
      },
      "rev_n_3_fedb86_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_fedb86_1",
        "node_id": "n_3_fedb86",
        "thought_text": "Revised Thought-1: Topological Sorting with Bus Capacity Check\n\nThe issue arises because the current implementation does not account for the bus capacity constraint. We need to modify the approach to ensure that we do not exceed the bus capacity while satisfying all preferences. Instead of simply counting the number of nodes in the topological order, we should keep track of the number of participants added to the trip until adding another participant would exceed the bus capacity. If adding a participant would exceed the capacity, we stop adding more participants even if there are still nodes left in the topological order.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_3_fedb86_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_b88b819136",
        "created_at": 1794505669.9902592
      },
      "rev_n_4_b730be_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_b730be_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_b730be",
        "thought_text": "Iterative Grouping",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "7160770164424270536",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505679.020907
      },
      "rev_n_5_0460e8_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_0460e8_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_0460e8",
        "thought_text": "Graph Theory Approach",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "7160770164424270536",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505679.0209234
      },
      "rev_n_6_7dc375_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_7dc375_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_7dc375",
        "thought_text": "Dynamic Programming",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "7160770164424270536",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505679.0209322
      },
      "rev_n_4_b730be_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_b730be_1",
        "node_id": "n_4_b730be",
        "thought_text": "Thought-2: Iterative Grouping with DFS Traversal\n\nIterate through each participant and perform a depth-first search (DFS) to identify connected components based on the given preferences. Each connected component represents a group of participants who can join together. Track the size of each group and ensure that the total number of participants does not exceed the bus capacity. Finally, return the minimum of the sum of group sizes and the bus capacity as the maximum number of participants that can join.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "7160770164424270536",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_4_b730be_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_05eca3db66",
        "created_at": 1794505754.9993846
      },
      "rev_n_7_d99d81_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_7_d99d81_0",
        "node_id": "n_7_d99d81",
        "thought_text": "Use a union-find data structure to manage groups of participants who need each other to join. Iterate through the participants and merge groups based on their preferences. Finally, count the size of the largest connected component that fits within the bus capacity.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-2839966432333712642",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505769.244137
      },
      "rev_n_8_30c6b4_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_8_30c6b4_0",
        "node_id": "n_8_30c6b4",
        "thought_text": "Create an adjacency list to represent the relationships between participants. Use Depth-First Search (DFS) or Breadth-First Search (BFS) to explore connected components. For each component, check if its size is less than or equal to the bus capacity. Keep track of the maximum number of participants that can join without exceeding the bus limit.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-2839966432333712642",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505769.2441545
      },
      "rev_n_9_6d3299_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_9_6d3299_0",
        "node_id": "n_9_6d3299",
        "thought_text": "Sort the participants based on their preference numbers. Start with the participant who has the lowest preference number and try to include them in the trip. If including them does not exceed the bus capacity, add them and their preferred companion to the trip. Continue this process until no more participants can be added without exceeding the bus capacity.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-2839966432333712642",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505769.2441623
      },
      "rev_n_5_0460e8_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_0460e8_1",
        "node_id": "n_5_0460e8",
        "thought_text": "Graph Theory Approach: Represent the problem using a directed graph where each node represents a participant and there is a directed edge from node \\(i\\) to node \\(j\\) if participant \\(i\\) requires participant \\(j\\) to join. Use Depth-First Search (DFS) to identify connected components in the graph. Each connected component represents a group of participants who can join together. Select the largest possible subset of these groups such that the total number of participants does not exceed the bus capacity \\(k\\).",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "7160770164424270536",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_5_0460e8_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_523d75fb92",
        "created_at": 1794505802.5688334
      },
      "rev_n_6_7dc375_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_7dc375_1",
        "node_id": "n_6_7dc375",
        "thought_text": "Thought-2 (revised): Topological Sorting with Cycle Detection\n\nTo solve this problem, we need to determine the largest subset of participants who can join the trip without violating any preferences and staying within the bus capacity. This can be approached using topological sorting with cycle detection. We build a directed graph where each node represents a participant, and an edge from node \\(i\\) to node \\(j\\) indicates that participant \\(i\\) requires participant \\(j\\) to join. We then perform a topological sort to identify the largest independent set of nodes that can be included in the trip. If a cycle is detected, it means there is a conflict that cannot be resolved, and we need to handle it appropriately. Finally, we ensure that the size of the selected set does not exceed the bus capacity \\(k\\).",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "7160770164424270536",
